#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Managed-Nodes.rst:8
msgid "Managing node lifecycles - example"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Managed-Nodes.rst:10
msgid ""
"Managed lifecycles for nodes allow greater control over the state of the ROS "
"system. This example uses a simple talker/listener pair of managed nodes to "
"show how a managed lifecycle can be implemented and used. You can use the "
"example to understand and experiment with managing nodes in this way."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Managed-Nodes.rst:14
msgid "**Area: ROS-framework | Content-type: example | Experience: expert**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Managed-Nodes.rst:18
msgid "Contents"
msgstr "目次"

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Managed-Nodes.rst:21
msgid "Summary"
msgstr "摘要"

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Managed-Nodes.rst:23
msgid ""
"ROS 2 introduces the concept of managed nodes, also called lifecycle nodes. "
"These nodes can be used to ensure that resources are correctly initialised, "
"activated, deactivated, and cleaned up as the node moves between lifecycle "
"states. A common use case is nodes that control hardware, where devices such "
"as cameras, lidars, motor drivers, and other sensors and actuators must be "
"started, configured, and shut down in a controlled order."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/nodes/Working-with-nodes/Managed-Nodes.rst:27
msgid ""
"Using lifecycle nodes helps ensure hardware is only initialised when it is "
"ready, and is safely released during shutdown or error recovery. The "
"following packages enable you to implement these managed nodes: "
"`rclcpp_lifecycle <https://index.ros.org/p/rclcpp_lifecycle/>`__ "
"(implementation library) and `lifecycle_msgs <https://index.ros.org/p/"
"lifecycle_msgs/>`__ (interface definitions)."
msgstr ""

